{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>וְהִשִּׂיג לָכֶם דַּיִשׁ וְגוֹ׳.</b> This is the blessing of the grain after it has been harvested, in the manner that they of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 42b): one who comes to measure his threshing floor says, 'May it be His will,' etc. Behold, for there is a blessing from HaShem even after it has become a threshing floor, and this is His saying ״וְהִשִּׂיג לָכֶם דַּיִשׁ״ — that the produce increases up to this boundary. And see what I explained in parashat Behar on the verse ״וְעָשָׂת אֶת הַתְּבוּאָה״ (Leviticus 25:21)." "<b>וַאֲכַלְתֶּם וְגוֹ׳ לָשֹׂבַע.</b> This is the blessing of food in the stomach, as they of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim): he eats a morsel and it is blessed in his innards." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that nothing will befall them of what is customary among people — for when their prosperity and foods increase so greatly, their appetite becomes sated by it and they do not have the strength to eat to satiety — but rather ״וַאֲכַלְתֶּם לַחְמְכֶם לָשֹׂבַע״ — abundant eating to the point of satiety." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to what they of blessed memory said (Megillah 7b): ״רְוָחָא לִבְסוּמֵי שְׁכִיחָא״ — 'spice for fragrant oil is common.' And this is His saying ״וַאֲכַלְתֶּם וְגוֹ׳ <b>לָשֹׂבַע</b>״ — that your bread will be so palatable that even when they are satiated they will eat it." "<b>Further,</b> Scripture is precise in saying <b>לַחְמְכֶם</b> ('your bread'): that they will not need relish to flavor the bread with it, but the bread alone is pleasing to the soul to eat from it to satiety." "<b>Further</b>, it intends according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory (Gittin 70a), that gross eating is a deadly poison to the body, and HaShem promised that they would eat their bread to satiety — even if they are satiated they will eat and it will not harm them." "<b>Further</b>, it intends that since there are fruits that contain fat (shuman), as they said in the Mishnah (Sotah 48a): the taste of the fruits and the fat of the fruits — now, any food that contains fat, a person eats from it and feels satiety quickly, whereas any food that lacks fat, even though a person eats and fills his stomach, the sensation of satiety is not felt in his intestines — and these are things perceived by every perceptive person, as is known. And the verse informed us here in the statement <b>and you shall eat your bread to satiety</b> — meaning, even bread alone that comes forth from the earth, they will feel satiety upon eating it, because it contains fat that sustains the human heart." "<b>And you shall dwell securely in your land.</b> It intends that since all the blessings stated in this matter could potentially be fulfilled yet their goodness would not be discernible — such as if they were preoccupied with wars, and one year they plow and sow and another year there is no plowing or reaping, one year making up for the next — if so, there would be no recognition of the blessing. Therefore Scripture says: ״וִישַׁבְתֶּם לָבֶטַח בְּאַרְצְכֶם״ — nothing troubles you, and each and every year the stated blessing will be yours. And its saying <b>in your land</b> — meaning, that the entire world will recognize and know that it is your land and strangers have no share in it with you, and in this you will not have even anxiety, and you will dwell in security." "<b>Further</b>, it intends in His saying <b>and you shall dwell securely</b> — meaning that you will not exert yourselves in harvesting and grape-gathering, but strangers shall work and you shall sit upon the throne, and its saying ״לָבֶטַח״ — that there is no anxiety over the acquisition being in the hands of workers."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {7 "The phrase וְאֵין טוֹבָתָם טוֹבָה is understood here as 'their goodness would not be [recognized as] good'; the logic requires this reading given the immediately following explanation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:52.875908625Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}